{"modelId":"qwen2.5-1.5b-instruct-q4km","taskType":"compare","adaptiveRoutingUsed":false,"reasonCodes":["eval:fixed-model","retrieve:relevant-to-task","generate:fixed-qwen2.5-1.5b-instruct-q4km"],"retrievalUsed":true,"modelSwitches":0,"crossMessageModelSwitch":false,"modelResidency":"resident","modelLoadMs":0,"ttftMs":763.7609160000002,"generationLatencyMs":3019.8915829999987,"totalLatencyMs":3799.893916,"tokensGenerated":246,"tokPerSec":81.45987802503177,"peakRssBytes":1720500224,"outcome":"success","runId":"desktop-2026-09-26T21-11-57-520Z","evalSetVersion":"dataset-suggestions-v1","configId":"model:qwen2.5-1.5b-instruct-q4km","configLabel":"Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (Q4_K_M)","personalityId":"succinct","maxTokens":512,"queryId":"quill-q2-pt","category":"compare","query":"Compare um vírus e uma bactéria","answer":"Viruses and bacteria are both microorganisms, but they differ in several key aspects:\n\n1. Structure: Bacteria are prokaryotic, meaning they have a simple cell structure without a membrane-bound nucleus. In contrast, viruses are typically composed of a nucleic acid (DNA or RNA) envelope and capsid.\n\n2. Reproduction: Bacteria can reproduce through binary fission, splitting into two identical daughter cells. Viruses, however, must hijack the host cell's machinery to replicate.\n\n3. Host specificity: Bacteria are relatively specific to their host organisms, often causing diseases within their natural habitats. Viruses can infect a wide range of hosts, including plants, animals, and even humans.\n\n4. Immunity: While bacterial infections can lead to immunity in the host, viruses can create a persistent infection that requires ongoing treatment.\n\n5. Disease manifestation: Bacterial infections can range from mild to severe and can be treated with antibiotics. Viral infections can also vary in severity but are generally managed with antiviral medications or symptomatic treatments.\n\n6. Evolution: Bacteria evolve through mutation and natural selection. Viruses evolve through genetic recombination, mutation, and the exchange of genetic material between different viruses.","promptFormat":"chat-template","chatWrapper":"Qwen","reasoningTokens":0,"thoughtBudget":256,"firstTokenMs":763.7597080000005,"thinking":"model-default","retrievedTitles":["Gut microbiota","Vaccine","CRISPR","Antimicrobial resistance"],"expectedKbTitles":[],"expectedKbHit":null,"timedOut":false,"createdAt":1790457127791,"runner":"desktop","hardware":"Apple M4 metal","corpus":"bundled","retrievalMs":5.739375000000109,"seed":1234,"loadAvg1m":4.568359375}
